(LST2-90 — the file-reading `treating` example: "-" on the command line is
 substituted for "/dev/stdin" for that iteration, so the same sentence
 reads a named file or standard input. The recorded block is the
 NO-ARGUMENT run (docs/check-probes.sh passes none), where arguments's all
 is empty and the loop runs zero times. Hand-run with a filename it copies
 that file; hand-run with `-` and a pipe it copies stdin — both verified:
   printf 'from stdin\n' | ./p -     ->  out.txt contains "from stdin"
   ./p docs/ledger/probes/collections-b/fixtures/two-lines.txt
                                     ->  out.txt contains "alpha\nbeta"
 Note: the manual's version of this block omits the `content` buffer
 declaration, which is an "Unknown buffer: content" compile error; the
 declaration below is the minimum that makes the example runnable.
 Ran: VOX_CORE_PATH=/home/josj/scr/english/vox/coreasm /home/josj/scr/english/vox/target/release/vox LST2-90.vox -o p && ./p
 expected output:
   copied every argument
)
open a file for writing called output at "/tmp/lst2-90-out.txt".
a buffer called content is 256 bytes in size.
open a file for reading called source at each filename from arguments's all treating "-" as "/dev/stdin",
  read from source into content,
  write content to output,
  close source.
close output.
print "copied every argument".
